# Centralized text sorting

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 core rule.

## Use This When

- Keep this rule active whenever code orders HUMAN-FACING text — names, titles,
  labels, folder/file lists, table columns, picker options, autocomplete results.

## Stop Here If

- Stop here once the comparator choice is clear; this is a leaf rule.

## Descend To

- No sibling descent required. Apply the rule directly.

Order human-facing text through the ONE canonical collator, never an ad-hoc
comparator. The single source of truth is `@workspace/std`:

- `compareText(a, b)` — the comparator (case-insensitive, natural: `Intl.Collator`
  with `numeric: true`, `sensitivity: 'base'`). So `Item 2` sorts before `Item 10`,
  and `a` next to `A`.
- `byText(item => item.title)` — the key adapter: returns a comparator for
  `array.sort(...)`.
- `@workspace/ui/lib/sort` RE-EXPORTS both (so UI code may import them from there)
  and adds `textSortingFn` — set it as a TanStack table's
  `defaultColumn.sortingFn` so every text column sorts the human way with no
  per-column wiring.

This is not a style preference: it keeps every surface that lists the SAME data
ordered IDENTICALLY (e.g. a sidebar that maps a raw list and a canvas that
re-sorts it), and it mirrors the Postgres side, which must order the same text via
an ICU collation + the resolver `order_by` (the cross-tier half of the contract).

## Rule

```ts
// ✅ correct — the canonical collator
import { byText, compareText } from '@workspace/std';
// (or, in UI code, from '@workspace/ui/lib/sort', which re-exports them)

nodes.sort(byText((n) => n.title));
labels.sort(compareText);

// ✅ TanStack table — one wiring for every text column
import { textSortingFn } from '@workspace/ui/lib/sort';
// defaultColumn: { sortingFn: textSortingFn }

// ❌ forbidden — naive / ad-hoc comparators for human text
nodes.sort((a, b) => a.title.localeCompare(b.title));
labels.sort();                       // default lexicographic (ASCII, case-sensitive)
nodes.sort((a, b) => (a.title < b.title ? -1 : 1));
```

## Allowed exceptions (require explicit justification in a comment)

1. **Non-human / opaque keys** — ordering ids, hashes, enum codes, or timestamps
   where natural-language collation is meaningless (sort those by their own
   semantics — numeric, chronological, etc.).
2. **A different EXPLICIT locale/collation requirement** — when a surface must sort
   under a specific locale or rule that `compareText` does not model; document the
   requirement and the chosen comparator inline.

If neither applies, route the comparison through `@workspace/std`.
